{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>According to all its statutes</b> — it is written in Rashi's commentary: these are the commandments pertaining to its body — an unblemished male lamb a year old; <b>and according to all its ordinances</b> — these are the commandments applied to its body, for the seven days of matzah and for the removal of leaven. But this is a scribe's error. Rather, the commandments pertaining to its body — an unblemished male lamb a year old; applied to its body — roasted with fire, its head on its legs and on its innards; outside its body — matzah and removal of leaven, concerning which the verse does not speak here. And so in the case of the second Passover it is said: חֻקֹּתָיו וּמִשְׁפָּטָיו (Numbers 9:14), and matzah and leaven are associated with it in the house, yet it applies for only one day. And the verse abbreviated, saying: \"between the evenings you shall make it; according to all its statutes and according to all its ordinances you shall make it,\" to include the laws of eating that do not fall on that day mentioned in the verse, for it is to be eaten on the night that follows it, since He already stated there: \"and they shall eat the flesh on this night\" etc. (Exodus 12:8). And likewise: \"in the second month on the fourteenth day between the evenings they shall make it; upon matzot and bitter herbs they shall eat it\" (Numbers 9:11) — its meaning is: they shall eat it at the time of eating mentioned there in the commandment of the first Passover."], :book :numbers, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 9, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:03:48.490959670Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}